#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134571} :activity/id #uuid "c72a2713-294e-5bf3-a1ca-721b4a5a5f0a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404 :topic/id #uuid "46172e8e-6e68-554f-baaa-252a31973372" :topic/math-academy-id 2204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135175 :tutorial/content "We know how to find the square root of a perfect square. But how do we find square roots of numbers that are not perfect squares?\n\nFor example, what is the square root of $5$?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 5 = \\:\n$$\n\nEven though $5$ is not a perfect square, $\\sqrt{5}$ is still a number. Such numbers are called **surds**. Surds are a new type of number that *cannot be written as a whole number or regular fraction*!\n\nSurds can, however, be written as infinite, non-repeating decimals.\n\nWe can find a decimal approximation to the value of $\\sqrt 5$ using the **square root button** $\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}}$ on our calculator.\n\nDifferent calculator models work differently. However, one of the following sequences should work with your calculator:\n\n- Press $\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}}$, type $\\bbox[lightgray, 4pt]{\\:5\\:}$, and then press $\\bbox[lightgray, 4pt]{\\:=\\:}$.\n- Alternatively, you may need to type $\\bbox[lightgray, 4pt]{\\:5\\:}$ first, then press $\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}}$, followed by $\\bbox[lightgray, 4pt]{\\:=\\:}$.\n\nIf you find yourself stuck, search for an online video that shows how to use the square root button with your calculator model.\n\nWhen we do this, we get the following on our calculator's display.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5} = 2.236\\,067\\,977\n$$\n\nThe value given by the calculator is an *approximation* to the true value of $\\sqrt 5$. In reality, the digits continue forever, and the pattern of decimal digits does not repeat. It is *impossible* to express $\\sqrt 5$ precisely using decimals." :tutorial/id #uuid "e462feae-1235-5718-8b9d-5ec284e6d71b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8037 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91bdb3fa-af0d-57f2-b5a8-2c5dade5d3ce" :step/math-academy-id 132257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133501 :question/id #uuid "127ac025-aa32-505a-98d8-27721a37fdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-12829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c606764-287a-500f-aa91-e6a68bec8860" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134144}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Decimal Approximation of a Square Root Surd"} :step/id #uuid "c6632f15-dcfd-5f47-ab63-3c36a369fd9c" :step/math-academy-id 132258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135185 :tutorial/content "A number is *not* a surd if we can write it as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt 9$ is *not* a surd because it is a perfect square and can be expressed as a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 9 = \\sqrt{3^2} = 3\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{16}}\n$$\n\nis *not* a surd because it can be written as a regular fraction (i.e., as the ratio of two whole numbers):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{4 \\cdot 4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we cannot have surds involving square roots of negative numbers. In other words, the numbers\n\n$\\sqrt{-2}, \\qquad \\sqrt{-5}, \\qquad \\sqrt{-14}$\nare *not* surds because they are *not real numbers*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d377452-f2b4-5c72-80ce-d643f8f571be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8363 :tutorial/title "Numbers That Are Not Surds"} :step/id #uuid "69bc6dd4-7575-5aeb-a397-947a836138af" :step/math-academy-id 132261 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133500 :question/id #uuid "0b823033-1f76-5656-a284-b94f33f3e359" :question/math-academy-id "e-12828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6ecc42-d84a-584f-9ccc-d6a987c89394" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134420}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Integers"} :step/id #uuid "9ac709cd-552f-50aa-b69e-d983a3ec2e82" :step/math-academy-id 132262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133515 :question/id #uuid "12b5f86f-9e86-5c1b-8773-38e2b69a6417" :question/math-academy-id "e-13357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1e6bea-5399-5dba-b2b1-f59441915324" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "ad93c1bc-44bd-5e00-a24e-9f1402579cd1" :step/math-academy-id 132265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135176 :tutorial/content "Similarly, we can have surds involving the cube root sign ($\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$). For example,\n$\\sqrt[3]{2}, \\quad\\sqrt[3]{9}, \\quad \\sqrt[3]{11}, \\quad \\sqrt[3]{129}, \\quad \\ldots$\nBut $\\sqrt[3]{8}$ is **not** a surd since we can get rid of the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{8} = \\sqrt[3]{2^3} = 2\n$$\n\nMost calculators have a \"cube root button\" $\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}}$ that can be used to compute cube roots.\n\nNote that we can have surds involving cube roots ($\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) of negative numbers, such as\n$\\sqrt[3]{-2}, \\quad \\sqrt[3]{-5}, \\quad \\sqrt[3]{-14}$,\nbecause it's fine to take the cube root of a negative number." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5b34634-e24f-5780-9fda-2d39dc3347bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8038 :tutorial/title "Surds With Cube Roots"} :step/id #uuid "a48ef472-2382-5ca8-8e7b-babfbe25146f" :step/math-academy-id 132268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133502 :question/id #uuid "9e145904-a9bf-59fc-9070-339b0e133de9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "115ee606-48f2-5a63-a5c6-fd8e3e1dbec0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134501}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cube Root Surds"} :step/id #uuid "3a1a1a85-8f6a-50dd-977c-70e0ac0215bf" :step/math-academy-id 132269}] :activity/title "Surds" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134044}]}
